  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\n- Warming Up Into Wednesday\n\nWeak divergence and positive vorticity advection will keep the\nmostly cloudy skies in place into Wednesday morning. Southwest\nflow builds into Wednesday bringing warmer temperatures. Highs\ntoday in the mid 50s (north) to mid 60s (south) will increase into\nthe upper 60s to low 70s for Wednesday.\n\n- Rain Chances Return Thursday\n\nThe low level jet and increased moisture move in late Wednesday\nnight into Thursday bringing a chance for light rain to the area. \nBest chances (60 to 90 percent) will be along the lakeshore\nThursday morning and afternoon. Probabilities for a tenth of an \ninch or more are only 20 to 30 percent, therefore amounts should \nbe light. There is a slight hint for some instability, therefore \nan isolated rumble of thunder can't be ruled out. Dry conditions \nmove in for Friday with cooler temperatures in the 60s.\n\n- Additional Rain Chances This Weekend into Next Week\n\nThe overall pattern varies as seen in the cluster analysis heading\ninto the weekend and next week. Ensembles show chances increasing\nSaturday (20 to 30 percent) into Sunday (40 to 50 percent).\nSaturday could be hit and miss as there is a question as to how\nfast the dry air erodes. Based on ensembles any rain Saturday\nwould be light if it occurs.\n\nAs mentioned in the previous discussion, there continues to be a\nsignal for a deeper wave and associated storm system early next\nweek. However the exact timing, strength, and placement of the low\nare uncertain. This is seen in the different long range ensemble\nclusters. Where they do agree, is periods of rain are expected for\nnext week. Confidence in forecast will increase as we get closer \nwith observations sampling the incoming pattern better.",
